  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating JavaScript Conflicts**\n\nI\'m now diving into source [4], which highlights a specific JavaScript error: \"Cannot set property fetch of #<Window> which has only a getter.\" This seems to stem from conflicting libraries redefining global objects, potentially causing issues with `vConsole` and similar debugging tools. Further investigation is needed to determine the exact conflict.\n\n\n**Analyzing Fetch Setter Issues**\n\nOkay, I\'m now focusing on `window.fetch` and its setter. It looks like the AI Studio preview or a similar environment might inject scripts, leading to conflicts if `fetch` is overwritten without a setter. I\'m checking how `fetch` is defined on `window` and exploring whether making it configurable or writable upfront in `index.html` could resolve these issues and prevent the \"TypeError: Cannot set property fetch of #<Window> which has only a getter\" error.\n\n\n**Implementing Defensive Script**\n\nI\'ve crafted a defensive script to address the `fetch` setter issue. My plan is to add it to the `<head>` of `index.html`. It checks for the `fetch` descriptor on the `window` object and, if a setter is missing but configurable, creates one using `Object.defineProperty`. I am now running tests, and I am excited about the prospect of resolving the conflict.\n\n\n**Testing Property Definition**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a specific edge case. I\'m testing the ability to define a setter for `window.fetch` using `Object.defineProperty`. I have a defensive script prepared, that I am testing now. If successful, I\'ll be able to shadow existing implementations with a getter and setter. I plan to use this to capture calls to an altered `fetch` and handle them gracefully.\n\n\n**Confirming Setter Behavior**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that defining a setter for `window.fetch` doesn\'t throw the `TypeError`, and the new function works as expected. This resolves the `fetch` error and unblocks the wrapper. I\'m now checking for similar getter-only properties like `XMLHttpRequest` and `console` to see if the fix needs broader application. It looks like the AI Studio preview runtime is the trigger for the error, as it injects scripts that interact with the preview iframe.\n\n\n**Defining Fetch with a Setter**\n\nI\'m now implementing the defensive strategy directly within `index.html`. My approach involves immediately defining a getter and setter for `window.fetch` at the beginning of the `<head>`. This aims to capture and preserve the existing `fetch` implementation before any conflicting scripts are executed. I\'m focusing on ensuring this modification happens synchronously to prevent any further potential conflicts.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 5
      nanos: 591485352
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
