  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Locating Server Files**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that `server.ts` isn\'t in the root directory. I examined the output of `list_dir /` and found the usual suspects, including `index.html`, `package.json`, and `src/`. For now, the application starts with `vite` listening on port 3000 and bound to all interfaces.\n\n\n**Analyzing Architecture Options**\n\nI\'ve realized the `package.json`\'s `dev` script uses Vite on port 3000, bound to all interfaces. Now I\'m considering the architecture. The guidelines suggest a client-side SPA by default. Using `@google/genai` necessitates a server-side proxy for the Gemini API. Alternatively, a full-stack `server.ts` or an advanced WordPress article management assistant could be constructed.\n\n\n**Investigating Project Setup**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `package.json` file. I see `express`, `@google/genai`, and `dotenv` are already declared, which changes the game. Also, there\'s a `clean` script removing `server.js`, but this project uses `server.ts`! I remember needing to update the scripts if I make a custom `server.ts` file, and the process for that. Now I\'m considering if the AI assistant can work client-side with a built-in engine, with an optional server endpoint for the Gemini API.\n\n\n**Considering Built-in AI Engine**\n\nI\'m now thinking about the AI assistant\'s architecture again. I\'m focusing on the idea of a powerful, instant, zero-latency built-in WordPress Intelligence Engine. This engine would handle article creation in diverse formats, optimizing for SEO and readability. I\'m imagining it\'d also manage WordPress post states and offer category recommendations. The idea is to have it work on the client side, but also optionally allow for a server endpoint for the Gemini API.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Client-Side Implementation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a client-side solution to maintain the SPA default. I\'m focusing on features such as a featured image picker, URL and Alt Text validators, confirmation dialogs, and an interactive chat panel with multiple language options. I\'m prioritizing the user experience with quick action prompt chips and direct \"Apply to Editor\" options. I\'m also planning for Gemini API integration on the server, if requested, but only if necessary, to avoid disruption. I must provide exactly what\'s requested. I\'m very aware of not altering the dev server.\n\n\n**Defining Assistant Scope**\n\nI\'ve re-read the detailed user requirements, clarifying the project\'s focus. This assistant is specifically for WordPress article management within the admin panel, covering article creation, SEO optimization, content checks, and WordPress functionalities. The user emphasizes WordPress integration. I\'ve re-evaluated that a client-side SPA with optional server calls to Gemini is the only real option.\n\n\n**Integrating Assistant into Panel**\n\nI\'m now fully focused on the user\'s needs to integrate a WordPress Article Management Assistant. This requires adding a dedicated tab or a floating/docked panel within the admin dashboard. The conversational interface will support Hindi, Hinglish, and English, with a language preference toggle. Users can request different article formats, SEO audits, heading suggestions, and metadata generation. This confirms the user\'s focus on a client-side SPA with optional server calls to the Gemini API, I am sure of it!\n\n\n**Expanding Assistant\'s Capabilities**\n\nI\'m now integrating readability analysis (Flesch, sentence structure, passive voice), and WordPress article management functions (scheduling, status changes, deletions with confirmations). I\'m formatting outputs in markdown with headings, lists, and actionable buttons. I\'ve added progress updates for longer tasks and quick preset prompts in Hindi and English, fully in line with user requirements.\n\n\n**Defining Article Elements**\n\nI\'m now focusing on specific article elements for the assistant. I\'ll include content format selection, a structured WYSIWYG editor with formatting options, and post status controls with a scheduler. I\'m also including category and tag management, along with featured image and SEO meta data. Focus on alt text and character counters for SEO is crucial!\n\n\n**Adding SEO Insights**\n\nI\'m now integrating detailed SEO insights into the assistant. This includes Google search previews for mobile and desktop, displaying the title, URL, and description. I\'m also incorporating Rank Math or Yoast live SEO scores, complete with green/amber/red indicators, and checklists for focus keyword optimization in title, meta description, URL, headings, and content. I\'m also including content length, keyword density analysis, image alt-text checks, and internal/external link detection.\n\n\n**Implementing Post Actions**\n\nI\'m now implementing post filtering by status, category, and search. I\'ll add \"Edit\" actions to load articles with live SEO scores, and deletion confirmation modals for post management. Scheduled posts will have a visible countdown badge. I\'ll also add export functionality to WordPress JSON/WXR. I am going to update `src/types.ts` to cleanly support WordPress article fields, ensuring backward compatibility with current components.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 11
      nanos: 199131547
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
